WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:06.941
SANDEEP PARIKH: Today I want
to talk about the hybrid data

00:00:06.941 --> 00:00:07.440
lifecycle.

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:08.856
And I'll get into
what that really

00:00:08.856 --> 00:00:11.400
means, because the title
is a little bit misleading.

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:12.930
My name is Sandeep Parikh.

00:00:12.930 --> 00:00:15.390
I run the Google Cloud
solutions architecture team

00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:17.850
for Americas East.

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:20.820
What I want you to think about
and take away from this session

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:23.670
is really
understanding that data

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:25.830
lives in multiple environments.

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:28.620
It's not just on premise,
it's not just in cloud.

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:30.060
It's kind of in both places.

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:31.690
And even in on
premise scenarios,

00:00:31.690 --> 00:00:33.905
you might have a sort
of core infrastructure,

00:00:33.905 --> 00:00:35.280
you might have
private cloud, you

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:37.290
might have other
virtualised infrastructure.

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:39.570
The idea is that data
is kind of spread out

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:40.789
in a bunch of places.

00:00:40.789 --> 00:00:42.330
And what I want to
do is kind of take

00:00:42.330 --> 00:00:44.910
that thought
process, particularly

00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:47.220
from the on premise
perspective, and map that

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:49.530
to what we think of
as the data lifecycle

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:53.800
through four applied scenarios,
or practical scenarios.

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:57.030
As I said earlier
when we opened,

00:00:57.030 --> 00:00:58.722
it's kind of a hybrid world.

00:00:58.722 --> 00:01:00.930
Whatever you want to call
it-- hybrid, heterogeneous,

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:01.915
multi cloud--

00:01:01.915 --> 00:01:04.290
you've got applications and
infrastructure kind of spread

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:07.260
out across a number of
different environments.

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:10.230
How many of you have a
scenario similar to this

00:01:10.230 --> 00:01:12.510
that you're running into
at your own organizations?

00:01:12.510 --> 00:01:13.889
You've got multiple
environments.

00:01:13.889 --> 00:01:15.930
You've got data spread
out across different ones.

00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:18.390
Each one is sort of satisfying
different workloads.

00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:21.270
The problem is it's hard to
wrap your hands around all

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:23.130
these things in one place.

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:24.990
So how do you tackle
those problems

00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:28.490
as it relates to things
like processing big data,

00:01:28.490 --> 00:01:31.320
or handling large
scale ingests coming in

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:32.944
from applications
or from devices?

00:01:32.944 --> 00:01:35.610
That's the sort of stuff we want
to try to unpack and figure out

00:01:35.610 --> 00:01:38.880
as best as possible.

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:42.067
Why do you even consider
going down this hybrid route?

00:01:42.067 --> 00:01:43.400
Why would you even look at this?

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:44.880
Everything is great on premise.

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:46.340
We should just
stay there forever.

00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:47.700
Let's never leave.

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:49.310
But the reality
is that there are

00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:52.280
a lot of challenges when you're
in an on-prem environment.

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:54.470
You've got things like
maxed out resources.

00:01:54.470 --> 00:01:56.690
That means if you want to
deploy new applications,

00:01:56.690 --> 00:01:59.030
if you want to deploy new
features or functionality,

00:01:59.030 --> 00:02:00.680
you've got to pull
those resources back

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:03.500
from something else, and
perhaps pay a latency

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:04.850
or a throughput penalty.

00:02:04.850 --> 00:02:06.630
That's not a great option.

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:09.050
You might just have generic
sort of compute limitations.

00:02:09.050 --> 00:02:10.610
People might have
to wait in line

00:02:10.610 --> 00:02:13.520
to run big processing
jobs and whatnot.

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:15.470
And ultimately, what
that sort of adds up to

00:02:15.470 --> 00:02:17.690
is sort of the third
item, which is, the time

00:02:17.690 --> 00:02:20.300
it takes to kind of
get meaningful insight

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:21.912
and understanding
out of your data

00:02:21.912 --> 00:02:23.870
and deliver that back,
not only to the business

00:02:23.870 --> 00:02:26.067
but to your end
customer, is pretty long.

00:02:26.067 --> 00:02:27.650
You're trying to get
out of this cycle

00:02:27.650 --> 00:02:29.774
where you're bumping up
against the ceiling of what

00:02:29.774 --> 00:02:30.791
you have access to.

00:02:30.791 --> 00:02:33.290
And you want to be able to get
information and understanding

00:02:33.290 --> 00:02:35.350
back out to every part
of the organization

00:02:35.350 --> 00:02:39.240
and, again, to your customers,
as quickly as possible.

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:41.190
So what are the
approaches you could take?

00:02:41.190 --> 00:02:43.689
So the one that people always
think we're going to tell them

00:02:43.689 --> 00:02:45.660
is, just pick up everything,
put it in a truck,

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:47.445
and drive it over to GCP.

00:02:47.445 --> 00:02:49.320
And while that's perfectly
fine if you really

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:52.370
want to go down that road, it's
not without its challenges.

00:02:52.370 --> 00:02:54.330
And I could trivialize
a bunch of the details,

00:02:54.330 --> 00:02:56.791
but the reality is that that
approach is really hard.

00:02:56.791 --> 00:02:59.040
If you try to pick all that
stuff up today and move it

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:01.259
to the cloud, you're going
to have trouble, right?

00:03:01.259 --> 00:03:02.550
People are going to be unhappy.

00:03:02.550 --> 00:03:03.633
Things are going to break.

00:03:03.633 --> 00:03:05.160
Ultimately what
might happen is, you

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:06.889
might drop data on the floor.

00:03:06.889 --> 00:03:09.180
You might build one off
migration processes that aren't

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:10.560
going to scale over time.

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:12.782
You're going to run into
a lot of rough edges.

00:03:12.782 --> 00:03:14.740
And frankly, you're just
not going to be happy.

00:03:14.740 --> 00:03:17.610
So instead of trying to say
let's move everything over,

00:03:17.610 --> 00:03:21.150
go whole hog right off the
gate, instead let's find ways

00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:22.920
to sort of bridge the gap.

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:25.330
Let's move certain
workloads that make sense.

00:03:25.330 --> 00:03:27.150
Let's try to find
an easy on ramp

00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:30.210
and let the cloud be
your place for scale,

00:03:30.210 --> 00:03:32.850
new applications,
compelling functionality

00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:34.890
that you couldn't do
because you're maxed out

00:03:34.890 --> 00:03:37.990
in on premise scenarios.

00:03:37.990 --> 00:03:39.534
So what is the data lifecycle?

00:03:39.534 --> 00:03:41.700
At least, what do we think
of the data lifecycle as?

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:43.610
Well, it's basically
four main steps.

00:03:43.610 --> 00:03:45.670
You've got ingest, and
that's data coming out

00:03:45.670 --> 00:03:48.160
of applications, data
coming out of devices,

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:49.870
mobile applications,
what have you.

00:03:49.870 --> 00:03:51.870
But that data is coming
into your infrastructure

00:03:51.870 --> 00:03:52.970
in some meaningful way.

00:03:52.970 --> 00:03:53.550
So the first thing
you're going to do

00:03:53.550 --> 00:03:54.760
is, you're going to
capture all that stuff

00:03:54.760 --> 00:03:56.230
and you're going to
store it somewhere.

00:03:56.230 --> 00:03:58.188
You might store that in
a variety of databases.

00:03:58.188 --> 00:04:01.130
It could be anything to
like objects, log files,

00:04:01.130 --> 00:04:03.970
into something like an object
storage infrastructure.

00:04:03.970 --> 00:04:05.140
It could be relational data.

00:04:05.140 --> 00:04:08.230
It could be semi-structured
and go into either key value,

00:04:08.230 --> 00:04:11.575
document, wide column
database infrastructure.

00:04:11.575 --> 00:04:13.700
And then you've got to do
something with that data.

00:04:13.700 --> 00:04:15.010
You want to actually
churn through it

00:04:15.010 --> 00:04:16.510
all in some meaningful
way and start

00:04:16.510 --> 00:04:18.470
extracting a little
bit of value out of it.

00:04:18.470 --> 00:04:19.480
So you want to go
through that step

00:04:19.480 --> 00:04:21.250
of processing all the
data, joining things

00:04:21.250 --> 00:04:23.710
together, de-normalizing
things in some cases,

00:04:23.710 --> 00:04:24.627
and then analyzing it.

00:04:24.627 --> 00:04:26.043
And then you get
to the last step,

00:04:26.043 --> 00:04:28.377
where you get to really explore
and visualize that data.

00:04:28.377 --> 00:04:29.918
And that's where
you start generating

00:04:29.918 --> 00:04:32.680
that really deep intelligence
about what your organization is

00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:34.420
really doing.

00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:38.744
So if you take those four steps
and you map them on to four

00:04:38.744 --> 00:04:40.410
what I call, kind of
practical scenarios

00:04:40.410 --> 00:04:42.490
that we talk about a
lot with customers,

00:04:42.490 --> 00:04:43.540
what does that look like?

00:04:43.540 --> 00:04:46.320
So the first scenario
is kind of basic.

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:48.580
You've got on prime
relational infrastructure.

00:04:48.580 --> 00:04:51.570
But you need to adopt a
data warehouse approach.

00:04:51.570 --> 00:04:54.134
You need something that's going
to let you scale out a lot,

00:04:54.134 --> 00:04:55.800
and that might not
just be one database,

00:04:55.800 --> 00:04:58.430
and frankly it probably
isn't just one database.

00:04:58.430 --> 00:05:03.780
It's probably a dozen or so
disparate, distinct sources

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:05.145
of data, distinct data types.

00:05:05.145 --> 00:05:07.020
But you've got to put
all that stuff together

00:05:07.020 --> 00:05:09.180
in order to get an
understanding of your business

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:10.740
from a number of
different angles.

00:05:10.740 --> 00:05:14.540
So one is, again, moving on-prem
relational to a data warehouse.

00:05:14.540 --> 00:05:16.830
And that's kind of
under the Process phase

00:05:16.830 --> 00:05:21.354
and under the Explore phase
of the data lifecycle.

00:05:21.354 --> 00:05:23.520
The next one is migrating
Hadoop and Spark workloads

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:24.416
to the cloud.

00:05:24.416 --> 00:05:26.040
This is usually easy,
low hanging fruit

00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:27.790
for a lot of people,
because they can just

00:05:27.790 --> 00:05:30.382
pick up their jobs and
move them somewhere else.

00:05:30.382 --> 00:05:32.340
But we're trivializing
a pretty critical detail

00:05:32.340 --> 00:05:34.740
which is, those big data
deployments on premise

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:36.294
usually have a lot of data.

00:05:36.294 --> 00:05:37.710
And it's not like
you can turn off

00:05:37.710 --> 00:05:40.140
the sources of data one day,
move everything, and turn it

00:05:40.140 --> 00:05:41.100
back on.

00:05:41.100 --> 00:05:43.770
That data is coming into the
system on a regular basis.

00:05:43.770 --> 00:05:46.200
So you have to find a way
to move everything once,

00:05:46.200 --> 00:05:50.100
in one fell swoop, and get that
ongoing, incremental transfer

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:51.230
to make sense.

00:05:51.230 --> 00:05:53.640
And that falls into the
Process and Analyze phase

00:05:53.640 --> 00:05:55.194
of the lifecycle.

00:05:55.194 --> 00:05:56.610
The other one I
want to talk about

00:05:56.610 --> 00:05:59.880
is cloud applications
plus on-prem data.

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:01.530
Listen, I'll be
honest with you, not

00:06:01.530 --> 00:06:04.020
every data source, not
every application, frankly,

00:06:04.020 --> 00:06:05.430
is a good fit for cloud.

00:06:05.430 --> 00:06:06.870
There is a lot of
stuff that might

00:06:06.870 --> 00:06:09.100
have to stay on prem,
for a variety of reasons.

00:06:09.100 --> 00:06:10.080
It could be compliance.

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:13.200
It could be in-depth security
concerns you might have.

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:15.870
It could be other
business requirements

00:06:15.870 --> 00:06:17.332
that we might not
even know about.

00:06:17.332 --> 00:06:19.290
But the idea is that
there are some things that

00:06:19.290 --> 00:06:20.220
have to stay there.

00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:24.270
So, how do I enable development
teams to deliver new features

00:06:24.270 --> 00:06:27.810
and functionality to a wide
variety of customers, but still

00:06:27.810 --> 00:06:30.152
having access to that
data that sits on prem?

00:06:30.152 --> 00:06:32.610
How do I front that for them
so they can go build something

00:06:32.610 --> 00:06:35.160
compelling while still not
really upsetting the balance

00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:37.322
of what's here on premise?

00:06:37.322 --> 00:06:39.030
So that's kind of in
the Ingest and Store

00:06:39.030 --> 00:06:40.839
phase of the lifecycle.

00:06:40.839 --> 00:06:42.630
And the last one is
kind of the same thing.

00:06:42.630 --> 00:06:45.720
Inset at the Ingest and
Store phase, but think back

00:06:45.720 --> 00:06:47.310
to those on-prem
challenges, where

00:06:47.310 --> 00:06:48.900
you're maxed out on resources.

00:06:48.900 --> 00:06:52.710
What if you have an insanely
great IoT idea tomorrow?

00:06:52.710 --> 00:06:55.400
You've got this compelling--
hilariously compelling

00:06:55.400 --> 00:06:58.320
connected light bulb, and you
want to get these things out.

00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:01.020
You're talking about millions
and millions of households

00:07:01.020 --> 00:07:03.450
with 10, 20, 30 bulbs in there.

00:07:03.450 --> 00:07:05.126
That's a lot of data.

00:07:05.126 --> 00:07:06.750
That's a lot of events
being generated.

00:07:06.750 --> 00:07:08.010
That's a lot of on
and off switches

00:07:08.010 --> 00:07:09.060
that are controlling
these things, that

00:07:09.060 --> 00:07:10.161
are pushing data through.

00:07:10.161 --> 00:07:11.910
You might not have
enough infrastructure--

00:07:11.910 --> 00:07:14.640
you might not have enough
infrastructure to actually take

00:07:14.640 --> 00:07:15.720
all that data in.

00:07:15.720 --> 00:07:16.886
So where is it going to go?

00:07:16.886 --> 00:07:18.510
You've got to put
that stuff somewhere.

00:07:18.510 --> 00:07:21.060
But you've also got to
remember to enable the business

00:07:21.060 --> 00:07:22.290
to learn from that.

00:07:22.290 --> 00:07:24.360
There might be internal
applications, again,

00:07:24.360 --> 00:07:26.980
living on prem, that
need access to this data.

00:07:26.980 --> 00:07:32.460
So how do you facilitate that
circuitous lifecycle there

00:07:32.460 --> 00:07:33.960
around where the
data is going to go

00:07:33.960 --> 00:07:35.415
and how you get access to it?

00:07:35.415 --> 00:07:36.790
So those are the
four workloads I

00:07:36.790 --> 00:07:38.880
want to talk about a
little bit in-depth,

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:42.030
and how you tackle
each one of them.

00:07:42.030 --> 00:07:45.150
The important thing to remember
is that in all these scenarios,

00:07:45.150 --> 00:07:48.532
you've got to be heterogeneous,
hybrid, multi cloud, whatever.

00:07:48.532 --> 00:07:50.865
You've got to be friendly to
this concept, which means--

00:07:50.865 --> 00:07:53.240
and again, I'm not going to
go through all this right now

00:07:53.240 --> 00:07:56.460
in this talk, but definitely
come find me afterwards.

00:07:56.460 --> 00:07:59.400
There are a lot of concerns
that are at the foundation level

00:07:59.400 --> 00:07:59.940
there.

00:07:59.940 --> 00:08:01.590
How do you handle security?

00:08:01.590 --> 00:08:04.417
How do you handle
governance, ownership?

00:08:04.417 --> 00:08:06.750
Who has identity and access
control to all these things?

00:08:06.750 --> 00:08:08.958
Those are all really important
things to think about.

00:08:08.958 --> 00:08:12.300
And it's critical that you
work through those open items

00:08:12.300 --> 00:08:13.680
before you go down this road.

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:15.221
Because once you do
that, then you've

00:08:15.221 --> 00:08:17.630
opened the door to
scaling on both sides.

00:08:17.630 --> 00:08:19.380
But again, you've got
to take on this sort

00:08:19.380 --> 00:08:22.581
of heterogeneous lifestyle
approach to the world.

00:08:22.581 --> 00:08:25.080
And then frankly, you've got
to have this thing be scalable,

00:08:25.080 --> 00:08:26.538
because otherwise
what's the point?

00:08:26.538 --> 00:08:28.814
If you can't scale it
on a cloud environment

00:08:28.814 --> 00:08:30.480
or in a public cloud,
then this approach

00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:33.860
really isn't going to work
in any of these cases.

00:08:33.860 --> 00:08:36.159
So let's dig into each one
of those practical scenarios

00:08:36.159 --> 00:08:38.927
and walk through what
they look like tactically.

00:08:38.927 --> 00:08:40.510
So the first one is
on-prem relational

00:08:40.510 --> 00:08:42.684
to cloud data warehouse.

00:08:42.684 --> 00:08:43.600
So it's really simple.

00:08:43.600 --> 00:08:45.450
Just do this.

00:08:45.450 --> 00:08:46.840
And that's it, the talk is done.

00:08:46.840 --> 00:08:48.400
No, I'm kidding.

00:08:48.400 --> 00:08:51.820
The idea is, you've got to get
database-- and frankly, again,

00:08:51.820 --> 00:08:55.030
databases, but I didn't want
to fill up too many icons

00:08:55.030 --> 00:08:55.930
on the slide, but--

00:08:55.930 --> 00:08:57.310
you've got multiple databases.

00:08:57.310 --> 00:08:59.310
You want to get them to
something like BigQuery,

00:08:59.310 --> 00:09:01.210
because you want to be
able to do ad hoc SQL.

00:09:01.210 --> 00:09:02.350
You want to explore that data.

00:09:02.350 --> 00:09:04.391
You want to look at it
normalized, de-normalized.

00:09:04.391 --> 00:09:05.980
You want to cut it
up and slice it up.

00:09:05.980 --> 00:09:08.650
You want to build compelling
reporting and dashboards

00:09:08.650 --> 00:09:11.110
on top of all of your
disparate data sources.

00:09:11.110 --> 00:09:14.552
Well, how do you go from
on-prem to cloud in that case?

00:09:14.552 --> 00:09:16.260
So the first thing
that we're going to do

00:09:16.260 --> 00:09:18.330
is, we're going to put
Apache Beam in here.

00:09:18.330 --> 00:09:22.420
And I'll talk a little bit
about why Beam is a good choice.

00:09:22.420 --> 00:09:25.916
So, Beam is a very modern
approach to data processing.

00:09:25.916 --> 00:09:27.040
It's very pipeline centric.

00:09:27.040 --> 00:09:30.250
So as data comes into it
the pipeline you create,

00:09:30.250 --> 00:09:32.294
you can apply a series
of operations to it.

00:09:32.294 --> 00:09:33.710
And at any point
in that pipeline,

00:09:33.710 --> 00:09:36.085
you can break out and dump
things wherever you want to.

00:09:36.085 --> 00:09:37.960
But the idea is, you
can have a pipeline that

00:09:37.960 --> 00:09:39.730
starts with multiple
data sources,

00:09:39.730 --> 00:09:41.500
and funnel them
through, and again, do

00:09:41.500 --> 00:09:43.672
things like in-flight
aggregation,

00:09:43.672 --> 00:09:45.130
if there's time
fields in there you

00:09:45.130 --> 00:09:46.780
can start windowing
some of that data.

00:09:46.780 --> 00:09:50.890
Basically, you can ETL that data
into whatever format it takes.

00:09:50.890 --> 00:09:53.230
But what's really
interesting about Apache Beam

00:09:53.230 --> 00:09:56.080
is that it unifies the model
between batch processing

00:09:56.080 --> 00:09:57.580
and stream processing.

00:09:57.580 --> 00:10:00.250
So, for example, today the
problem you might be solving

00:10:00.250 --> 00:10:01.990
is, I've got a
bunch of databases

00:10:01.990 --> 00:10:04.840
and I want to ETL them into some
kind of cohesive data warehouse

00:10:04.840 --> 00:10:05.590
structure.

00:10:05.590 --> 00:10:07.000
And that might be a batch job.

00:10:07.000 --> 00:10:09.790
But tomorrow, you might
say, I'm moving that to--

00:10:09.790 --> 00:10:11.957
I'm going to start including
streaming data sources.

00:10:11.957 --> 00:10:14.206
The great thing about Beam
is that there's pretty much

00:10:14.206 --> 00:10:15.490
no change to your code.

00:10:15.490 --> 00:10:17.770
You effectively have to
change the inputs to your code

00:10:17.770 --> 00:10:20.290
and say this is a streaming
source versus a batch source.

00:10:20.290 --> 00:10:22.540
And your code just works
the same way at that point.

00:10:22.540 --> 00:10:23.560
There's no real change.

00:10:23.560 --> 00:10:26.929
And that's a really powerful
concept to work through.

00:10:26.929 --> 00:10:28.720
Beam is also really
compelling because it's

00:10:28.720 --> 00:10:31.480
portable across a number
of runtime environments.

00:10:31.480 --> 00:10:33.640
If you've already
got a YARN cluster

00:10:33.640 --> 00:10:36.490
running MapReduce
or Spark on premise,

00:10:36.490 --> 00:10:39.010
Beam can sit right on top of
Spark and run on top of Spark.

00:10:39.010 --> 00:10:41.206
Or it can actually run
on top of Link as well.

00:10:41.206 --> 00:10:43.580
And then when you want to get
into the cloud environment,

00:10:43.580 --> 00:10:46.270
Beam can actually run in
the cloud dataflow service

00:10:46.270 --> 00:10:47.590
that Google offers.

00:10:47.590 --> 00:10:49.710
And I'll talk a little
bit about that as well.

00:10:49.710 --> 00:10:51.400
And right now, as
of today actually,

00:10:51.400 --> 00:10:54.010
Beam supports building
pipelines in Java or Python.

00:10:54.010 --> 00:10:56.530
So you have that ability
to mix and match depending

00:10:56.530 --> 00:11:00.840
on what makes sense for the
skill set that your teams have.

00:11:00.840 --> 00:11:02.760
So Apache Beam is
really interesting.

00:11:02.760 --> 00:11:06.637
If you go way, way, way back
into the early 2000's, back

00:11:06.637 --> 00:11:08.220
when Google only
wrote research papers

00:11:08.220 --> 00:11:10.303
and didn't really build
the public facing products

00:11:10.303 --> 00:11:12.750
around them, MapReduce is
one of the first papers.

00:11:12.750 --> 00:11:15.225
I would say MapReduce and
GFS, Google File System,

00:11:15.225 --> 00:11:17.100
were one of the first
two papers that I think

00:11:17.100 --> 00:11:19.680
really came out
and helped kick off

00:11:19.680 --> 00:11:22.410
a whole host of other
research at Google,

00:11:22.410 --> 00:11:24.720
and a bunch of things
that ultimately became

00:11:24.720 --> 00:11:26.549
part of the Hadoop ecosystem.

00:11:26.549 --> 00:11:28.590
So if you think about,
again, Google File System,

00:11:28.590 --> 00:11:31.230
that was basically
what became HDFS.

00:11:31.230 --> 00:11:34.620
The MapReduce paper is what
became Hadoop and the follow

00:11:34.620 --> 00:11:38.580
on between MapReduce 1.0 and
the YARN approach that came

00:11:38.580 --> 00:11:40.220
and Hadoop 2.

00:11:40.220 --> 00:11:42.530
But there's a strong
lineage here for Apache Beam

00:11:42.530 --> 00:11:43.610
to build on top of.

00:11:43.610 --> 00:11:45.680
And in fact, over
the last few years,

00:11:45.680 --> 00:11:48.470
Google has actually moved away
from MapReduce internally,

00:11:48.470 --> 00:11:49.880
into a number of other tools.

00:11:49.880 --> 00:11:52.400
Not just a new distributed
storage approach, but also

00:11:52.400 --> 00:11:54.800
a distributed compute
and processing approach,

00:11:54.800 --> 00:11:58.220
and the product of all of those
things over the last few years

00:11:58.220 --> 00:12:00.800
was something we
called Cloud Dataflow.

00:12:00.800 --> 00:12:03.752
Cloud Dataflow came out, I
think maybe 18 months ago.

00:12:03.752 --> 00:12:04.460
And it was great.

00:12:04.460 --> 00:12:05.720
It's a very powerful tool.

00:12:05.720 --> 00:12:09.710
But what we saw was the need
for adopting a more open source

00:12:09.710 --> 00:12:12.800
mindset, because we want to
see Dataflow be successful.

00:12:12.800 --> 00:12:13.650
We want to see--

00:12:13.650 --> 00:12:15.350
but even more than
Dataflow, we want

00:12:15.350 --> 00:12:16.910
to see the idea of
batch and stream

00:12:16.910 --> 00:12:19.862
processing become successful
from this unified code base.

00:12:19.862 --> 00:12:21.320
So what we did is,
we took Dataflow

00:12:21.320 --> 00:12:23.390
and we split the two things out.

00:12:23.390 --> 00:12:25.280
One is the service
that we run for you,

00:12:25.280 --> 00:12:27.260
and the other is the
SDK portion, which

00:12:27.260 --> 00:12:28.910
is what became Apache Beam.

00:12:28.910 --> 00:12:30.770
So now what you
can do is, you can

00:12:30.770 --> 00:12:33.410
write your pipelines in
Apache Beam, fully open source

00:12:33.410 --> 00:12:37.910
under Apache, and then run them,
Spark, Flink, or on Dataflow.

00:12:37.910 --> 00:12:40.580
So you get a ton of
portability for runtime, which

00:12:40.580 --> 00:12:42.920
is a really powerful concept
because again, as you think

00:12:42.920 --> 00:12:45.290
about this world where
you started from on-prem

00:12:45.290 --> 00:12:47.612
and you're slowly making
your way into the cloud,

00:12:47.612 --> 00:12:49.820
you're not going to dump
those original data sources.

00:12:49.820 --> 00:12:51.819
They just might be located
in a different place.

00:12:51.819 --> 00:12:53.420
They might be running
on GCP, inside

00:12:53.420 --> 00:12:57.380
of things like Cloud SQL,
cloud storage, BigTable, Cloud

00:12:57.380 --> 00:12:59.137
Datastore, Cloud Spanner even.

00:12:59.137 --> 00:13:01.220
But you still want to use
the same Beam pipelines.

00:13:01.220 --> 00:13:02.761
So instead of running
them on premise

00:13:02.761 --> 00:13:04.580
on top of a Spark
or Flink cluster,

00:13:04.580 --> 00:13:07.370
you can actually run those Beam
pipelines inside of Dataflow.

00:13:07.370 --> 00:13:10.410
So that's where-- that's some
of the history around that.

00:13:10.410 --> 00:13:12.050
Beam is actually,
again, pretty cool.

00:13:12.050 --> 00:13:14.060
It has four main
modes of operation.

00:13:14.060 --> 00:13:16.430
Very classically,
a big batch job,

00:13:16.430 --> 00:13:18.060
the ability to do
windowed batches.

00:13:18.060 --> 00:13:20.514
If you want to do it by
time or by data set sizes.

00:13:20.514 --> 00:13:22.472
You can also do things
like streaming again out

00:13:22.472 --> 00:13:25.944
of the same codebase, or
streaming plus accumulation,

00:13:25.944 --> 00:13:27.360
which is a little
bit in the weeds

00:13:27.360 --> 00:13:30.319
so we won't spend a
ton of time on it.

00:13:30.319 --> 00:13:32.110
So this is an example
of the Beam pipeline.

00:13:32.110 --> 00:13:33.680
And on the left I've
got the batch version,

00:13:33.680 --> 00:13:35.350
on the right I've got
the streaming version.

00:13:35.350 --> 00:13:36.850
And if you look,
I only really had

00:13:36.850 --> 00:13:39.462
to change three lines of
code to make this work.

00:13:39.462 --> 00:13:40.920
There's a little
bit left out here,

00:13:40.920 --> 00:13:42.700
I've glazed over a
handful of details.

00:13:42.700 --> 00:13:44.290
But the important
thing is that I

00:13:44.290 --> 00:13:47.020
went from a file based input,
which is the batch source,

00:13:47.020 --> 00:13:50.650
and a file based output, to a
streaming input and a streaming

00:13:50.650 --> 00:13:53.260
output, just by changing
the inputs and outputs.

00:13:53.260 --> 00:13:54.760
Nothing else in the
middle there had

00:13:54.760 --> 00:13:56.800
to change as far as
the functionality goes.

00:13:59.922 --> 00:14:02.380
So when you think about running
Beam pipelines in Dataflow,

00:14:02.380 --> 00:14:03.380
what are the advantages?

00:14:03.380 --> 00:14:05.170
Why would you even
go down this road?

00:14:05.170 --> 00:14:06.795
And it's really
important to understand

00:14:06.795 --> 00:14:09.970
that we take the idea
of managed services

00:14:09.970 --> 00:14:11.780
and managed infrastructure
very seriously.

00:14:11.780 --> 00:14:14.440
We think it's a really critical
component of how Cloud should

00:14:14.440 --> 00:14:16.360
work and be built.
And we want to see

00:14:16.360 --> 00:14:18.280
people be successful
without having

00:14:18.280 --> 00:14:21.160
to sit there and fiddle
with individual VMs

00:14:21.160 --> 00:14:23.335
and worry about scale
up and scale down

00:14:23.335 --> 00:14:24.460
in a lot of these examples.

00:14:24.460 --> 00:14:27.190
So Dataflow lets you
take a Beam pipeline.

00:14:27.190 --> 00:14:28.990
You push it to the
Dataflow service.

00:14:28.990 --> 00:14:32.060
We scale up that pipeline,
and scale it down for you,

00:14:32.060 --> 00:14:33.920
depending on what
the input looks like.

00:14:33.920 --> 00:14:35.590
So if you're streaming
data in, and we

00:14:35.590 --> 00:14:37.930
see the volume of the
stream start going up,

00:14:37.930 --> 00:14:39.520
we'll add more infrastructure.

00:14:39.520 --> 00:14:41.320
As we see the volume
in the stream go down,

00:14:41.320 --> 00:14:42.790
we'll reduce the infrastructure.

00:14:42.790 --> 00:14:44.410
Just like the data
set size if you're

00:14:44.410 --> 00:14:46.810
doing batch mode,
if you see that you

00:14:46.810 --> 00:14:48.720
have a lot of data coming in--

00:14:48.720 --> 00:14:50.440
if you've selected
a data source that's

00:14:50.440 --> 00:14:52.140
got a lot of data
set size to it--

00:14:52.140 --> 00:14:54.350
we'll go ahead and give
you a much larger cluster.

00:14:54.350 --> 00:14:56.716
And you have the ability to
control min and max there,

00:14:56.716 --> 00:14:58.840
about how you want to handle
it and how quickly you

00:14:58.840 --> 00:15:00.172
need to get the data back.

00:15:00.172 --> 00:15:01.630
But the important
thing is that you

00:15:01.630 --> 00:15:04.270
don't have to go and
actually provision

00:15:04.270 --> 00:15:05.830
and configure those
virtual machines.

00:15:05.830 --> 00:15:07.132
We do all that for you.

00:15:07.132 --> 00:15:08.590
And then there's
other great things

00:15:08.590 --> 00:15:11.470
like a built in UI for
monitoring and understanding

00:15:11.470 --> 00:15:13.679
your pipeline and debugging it.

00:15:13.679 --> 00:15:16.220
And then we do a bunch of other
stuff on top of that as well.

00:15:16.220 --> 00:15:18.270
So, two things that
we've added recently--

00:15:18.270 --> 00:15:20.830
or that we've gone more
depth into recently,

00:15:20.830 --> 00:15:24.190
are templates and orchestration.

00:15:24.190 --> 00:15:26.620
With templates, you
can actually define

00:15:26.620 --> 00:15:28.780
a series of inputs
in your code that you

00:15:28.780 --> 00:15:32.150
want us to be able to templatize
and parameterize at runtime.

00:15:32.150 --> 00:15:34.690
So instead of having to push
your job every single time

00:15:34.690 --> 00:15:36.730
you want to run it
with new parameters,

00:15:36.730 --> 00:15:38.770
you can stage the
job once, tell us

00:15:38.770 --> 00:15:40.630
which parameters
can be adjusted,

00:15:40.630 --> 00:15:42.692
and then you can launch
this from either the UI

00:15:42.692 --> 00:15:44.900
or from the API just by
passing in the new parameters

00:15:44.900 --> 00:15:47.317
every single time.

00:15:47.317 --> 00:15:48.900
And I'll just jump
ahead for a second,

00:15:48.900 --> 00:15:51.180
but this is what it looks
like to create a template.

00:15:51.180 --> 00:15:52.650
You've got to add a
little bit of metadata

00:15:52.650 --> 00:15:54.608
into your code around
all the parameters you're

00:15:54.608 --> 00:15:55.300
going to supply.

00:15:55.300 --> 00:15:57.758
And then we can go ahead and
make it really easy to, again,

00:15:57.758 --> 00:16:01.540
run from a staging environment
very, very quickly.

00:16:01.540 --> 00:16:04.030
If you take that idea back to
Orchestration, what happens

00:16:04.030 --> 00:16:07.260
is you can start coming up
with really compelling ways

00:16:07.260 --> 00:16:09.790
to get your pipeline
up and running.

00:16:09.790 --> 00:16:12.430
If you've got a templatized
Dataflow job, or a Beam

00:16:12.430 --> 00:16:15.160
pipeline that you've saved
in Dataflow, and you want to,

00:16:15.160 --> 00:16:18.700
maybe, kick off a job every time
you get a pub/sub notification.

00:16:18.700 --> 00:16:20.650
Or you want to kick
off a job every time

00:16:20.650 --> 00:16:22.570
a new log file is put
into cloud storage,

00:16:22.570 --> 00:16:25.690
or certain rows are added
to a cloud SQL table.

00:16:25.690 --> 00:16:26.941
You can do that really easily.

00:16:26.941 --> 00:16:28.648
And you can orchestrate
it through things

00:16:28.648 --> 00:16:30.970
like App Engine with Cron,
or through Cloud Functions.

00:16:30.970 --> 00:16:32.428
And both of those
can actually just

00:16:32.428 --> 00:16:34.525
go ahead and kick off a
Dataflow job just for you.

00:16:34.525 --> 00:16:36.850
Again, without having to
stage and upload JAR files

00:16:36.850 --> 00:16:38.390
and push those things out.

00:16:38.390 --> 00:16:41.750
So it's a pretty
compelling way to run.

00:16:41.750 --> 00:16:44.710
On the template front, we have
a couple of baked examples

00:16:44.710 --> 00:16:47.250
that are in the docs, like
WordCount and Cloud Pub/Sub

00:16:47.250 --> 00:16:48.310
to BigQuery.

00:16:48.310 --> 00:16:50.770
WordCount is certainly
the Hello World

00:16:50.770 --> 00:16:52.547
of Apache Beam or Dataflow.

00:16:52.547 --> 00:16:54.130
But actually Cloud
Pub/Sub to BigQuery

00:16:54.130 --> 00:16:55.604
is a very common thing.

00:16:55.604 --> 00:16:57.520
We see people want to
take streaming pipelines

00:16:57.520 --> 00:16:59.061
and go straight to
BigQuery with just

00:16:59.061 --> 00:17:00.490
a little bit of transformation.

00:17:00.490 --> 00:17:04.149
And so that's an easy one
that you can try out as well.

00:17:04.149 --> 00:17:05.690
All right, so that
was the first one,

00:17:05.690 --> 00:17:07.520
moving on-prem
relational to cloud.

00:17:07.520 --> 00:17:10.849
And again, there's a ton of
detail under the covers there.

00:17:10.849 --> 00:17:12.829
So if anybody has
questions on this,

00:17:12.829 --> 00:17:15.440
I urge you to hit the mics
or find me after the talk.

00:17:15.440 --> 00:17:18.939
I'm happy to walk you through
some of the things I left out.

00:17:18.939 --> 00:17:21.230
So the next one is migrating
Hadoop and Spark workloads

00:17:21.230 --> 00:17:22.310
to the cloud.

00:17:22.310 --> 00:17:24.200
That's a very popular one.

00:17:24.200 --> 00:17:26.240
Migrating the Hadoop
and Spark part,

00:17:26.240 --> 00:17:29.272
to be totally honest with
you, trivial, super easy.

00:17:29.272 --> 00:17:30.980
Right You've already
got JAR files built.

00:17:30.980 --> 00:17:32.260
You might have some
supporting libraries

00:17:32.260 --> 00:17:33.230
that have to go with them.

00:17:33.230 --> 00:17:35.605
But ultimately, you just have
to deploy those jobs on us,

00:17:35.605 --> 00:17:38.930
as opposed to pointing them
to your local YARN master.

00:17:38.930 --> 00:17:40.230
That's not the hard part.

00:17:40.230 --> 00:17:42.380
The hard part is moving data.

00:17:42.380 --> 00:17:46.697
You've got gigabytes, petabytes
of data stored in HGFS.

00:17:46.697 --> 00:17:49.280
How do you actually, physically,
truck that over to us-- well,

00:17:49.280 --> 00:17:50.905
not physically, but
how do you actually

00:17:50.905 --> 00:17:53.270
move that data over to
us in a reasonable manner

00:17:53.270 --> 00:17:56.180
that again, not just one time--
because one time is easy.

00:17:56.180 --> 00:17:59.390
It's one time, plus
the ongoing transfer.

00:17:59.390 --> 00:18:03.042
And for that, we have a couple
of great partners in Attunity

00:18:03.042 --> 00:18:05.000
and Wandiscco-- who are
downstairs, by the way,

00:18:05.000 --> 00:18:06.252
on the first floor.

00:18:06.252 --> 00:18:08.210
They would love to talk
to you more about this.

00:18:08.210 --> 00:18:09.980
But basically,
they have software

00:18:09.980 --> 00:18:13.100
that you run on premise that
lets you facilitate the one

00:18:13.100 --> 00:18:16.100
time transfer, and the
ongoing incremental transfer.

00:18:16.100 --> 00:18:17.780
And that's really
important because,

00:18:17.780 --> 00:18:19.807
if you go back to
what I said earlier--

00:18:19.807 --> 00:18:21.890
and this is kind of a
personal philosophy of mine.

00:18:21.890 --> 00:18:25.550
I hate telling people to move
everything in one fell swoop.

00:18:25.550 --> 00:18:27.380
One fell swoop is not easy.

00:18:27.380 --> 00:18:29.690
it's pretty hard, and
it's a lot of pain.

00:18:29.690 --> 00:18:32.030
You're better off trying
to find an approach that

00:18:32.030 --> 00:18:34.220
lets you slowly
make the transition,

00:18:34.220 --> 00:18:35.930
and then lets you
run really fast.

00:18:35.930 --> 00:18:38.991
If you try to do everything at
once, things don't work well.

00:18:38.991 --> 00:18:41.240
You're much better off trying
to find an approach that

00:18:41.240 --> 00:18:43.902
lets you start slow
and keep running

00:18:43.902 --> 00:18:45.110
faster and faster and faster.

00:18:45.110 --> 00:18:48.240
And then over time, you can
get rid of the on premise HTFS

00:18:48.240 --> 00:18:50.000
cluster, once you've
made that transition

00:18:50.000 --> 00:18:52.100
and you've migrated data
sources and ingest over

00:18:52.100 --> 00:18:53.791
to Cloud as well.

00:18:53.791 --> 00:18:55.790
All right, so let's say
we've done this process.

00:18:55.790 --> 00:18:57.248
We've got the batch
transfer going.

00:18:57.248 --> 00:18:58.990
We got the incremental
transfer going.

00:18:58.990 --> 00:18:59.950
What does it look
like when you're

00:18:59.950 --> 00:19:02.533
starting to run Cloud Dataproc,
which is our management Hadoop

00:19:02.533 --> 00:19:04.370
and Spark service.

00:19:04.370 --> 00:19:05.900
Dataproc is kind of cool.

00:19:05.900 --> 00:19:08.840
We do a lot of work-- and this
is another core philosophy

00:19:08.840 --> 00:19:10.010
of Google Cloud--

00:19:10.010 --> 00:19:12.860
we do a lot of work to
separate storage and compute.

00:19:12.860 --> 00:19:15.320
We want them to be able
to scale independently.

00:19:15.320 --> 00:19:18.770
So instead of having
you run HDFS on GCP,

00:19:18.770 --> 00:19:20.510
we tell you to
run Cloud Storage.

00:19:20.510 --> 00:19:23.330
Cloud Storage is always
on, always available,

00:19:23.330 --> 00:19:25.460
all the nines you
need on availability.

00:19:25.460 --> 00:19:27.320
The idea is that you
do all your storage

00:19:27.320 --> 00:19:29.510
that you would typically
have done in HDFS, inside

00:19:29.510 --> 00:19:30.620
of Cloud Storage.

00:19:30.620 --> 00:19:34.100
And you spin up Cloud Dataproc
clusters for Hadoop and SPARC

00:19:34.100 --> 00:19:37.230
to actually do the compute
aspect of your job.

00:19:37.230 --> 00:19:39.600
Cloud Dataproc lets you
spin up compute jobs--

00:19:39.600 --> 00:19:41.310
or compute clusters,
I should say--

00:19:41.310 --> 00:19:42.950
in about 90 seconds.

00:19:42.950 --> 00:19:46.950
And that's true of clusters
that are anywhere from 10 nodes

00:19:46.950 --> 00:19:49.319
to 100 nodes, to 1,000
nodes, to even larger.

00:19:49.319 --> 00:19:50.860
And if you start to
think about that,

00:19:50.860 --> 00:19:54.330
1,000 nodes with 64 cores each.

00:19:54.330 --> 00:19:56.520
That's a lot of CPU
power you can get.

00:19:56.520 --> 00:19:59.520
Or if you're thinking of
Spark for a RAM perspective,

00:19:59.520 --> 00:20:02.139
that's up to 416 gigs
of RAM per instance

00:20:02.139 --> 00:20:03.180
that's running out there.

00:20:03.180 --> 00:20:04.920
So it's a pretty big number.

00:20:04.920 --> 00:20:07.600
You've got a lot of memory
headroom to work with.

00:20:07.600 --> 00:20:10.620
And those clusters actually do
spin up in about 90 seconds.

00:20:10.620 --> 00:20:13.710
It's probably on the
order of 70 to 90 seconds,

00:20:13.710 --> 00:20:15.510
but we say 90 just
in case there's

00:20:15.510 --> 00:20:17.190
a hiccup somewhere
in there to give us

00:20:17.190 --> 00:20:19.620
a little bit of head room.

00:20:19.620 --> 00:20:23.850
And Cloud Dataproc's got
built in monitoring, logging,

00:20:23.850 --> 00:20:24.870
that sort of thing.

00:20:24.870 --> 00:20:27.870
And the best part is
that you get to follow

00:20:27.870 --> 00:20:29.490
the same open source train.

00:20:29.490 --> 00:20:32.490
So if you're familiar with
the Apache Bigtop project,

00:20:32.490 --> 00:20:34.590
it's kind of this idea
where they sort of

00:20:34.590 --> 00:20:36.180
draw a line in the
sand around all

00:20:36.180 --> 00:20:39.030
the versions of the software and
call that a particular release.

00:20:39.030 --> 00:20:42.720
And it might be Hadoop 2.x
something, Spark 2.something,

00:20:42.720 --> 00:20:44.241
Pig, Hive, et cetera.

00:20:44.241 --> 00:20:45.240
That's a Bigtop release.

00:20:45.240 --> 00:20:47.250
So we follow the
Bigtop release train,

00:20:47.250 --> 00:20:51.404
which is similar to what
Hortonworks does as well.

00:20:51.404 --> 00:20:53.320
In practice, Dataproc
is actually kind of cool

00:20:53.320 --> 00:20:54.986
as well, because you
can resize clusters

00:20:54.986 --> 00:20:57.550
on the fly, including not just
the number of nodes in there,

00:20:57.550 --> 00:20:59.279
but things like attach
disks, or if you

00:20:59.279 --> 00:21:00.820
want to adjust the
way the networking

00:21:00.820 --> 00:21:02.960
layout is on your cluster.

00:21:02.960 --> 00:21:04.480
We also have custom VMs.

00:21:04.480 --> 00:21:07.150
So if you want to
skew higher on memory

00:21:07.150 --> 00:21:10.090
and lower on cores, for example,
like in the case of Spark

00:21:10.090 --> 00:21:11.864
workloads, that's
certainly possible.

00:21:11.864 --> 00:21:13.780
You just tell us the
shape of the VM you want,

00:21:13.780 --> 00:21:16.080
and we'll use that
for the worker pool.

00:21:16.080 --> 00:21:17.736
The third point--
and this is what

00:21:17.736 --> 00:21:19.860
I think is the most compelling
one about Dataproc--

00:21:19.860 --> 00:21:23.316
is the ability to use
preemptable virtual machines.

00:21:23.316 --> 00:21:24.690
So just by a show
of hands, who's

00:21:24.690 --> 00:21:26.820
heard of preemptables
in the room?

00:21:26.820 --> 00:21:27.850
OK, so a fair amount.

00:21:27.850 --> 00:21:31.550
So just real quick,
preemptables are super powerful.

00:21:31.550 --> 00:21:34.330
This is a machine that we can
give you for up to 24 hours.

00:21:34.330 --> 00:21:36.910
After that, we can take
it away at any time.

00:21:36.910 --> 00:21:40.750
But the idea is that it
runs at 80% off list price.

00:21:40.750 --> 00:21:43.330
There's no limitation
on configuration.

00:21:43.330 --> 00:21:47.590
It's the same exact VMs you get
access to, except we can just--

00:21:47.590 --> 00:21:51.160
we can preempt it, basically,
within a 24 hour window.

00:21:51.160 --> 00:21:54.190
Now, if you think about how
that maps back to your jobs,

00:21:54.190 --> 00:21:57.760
instead of running a job that
takes two days on 100 nodes,

00:21:57.760 --> 00:22:01.510
why not run that job that takes
less than a day on 200 nodes?

00:22:01.510 --> 00:22:02.410
Or 500 nodes?

00:22:02.410 --> 00:22:03.550
Or 1000 nodes?

00:22:03.550 --> 00:22:06.850
Because you're running with
a dramatically reduced cost,

00:22:06.850 --> 00:22:09.460
you can actually dramatically
scale up your cluster size

00:22:09.460 --> 00:22:11.439
and get the compute job
done that much faster.

00:22:11.439 --> 00:22:13.730
And on top of that, we actually
have permanent billing,

00:22:13.730 --> 00:22:16.630
which means every minute you
shave off that runtime means

00:22:16.630 --> 00:22:18.040
that much less you're spending.

00:22:18.040 --> 00:22:20.050
So it's an important
metric to start

00:22:20.050 --> 00:22:23.380
thinking about how to map
your compute jobs around.

00:22:23.380 --> 00:22:26.350
And then on top of-- the other
critical part of Dataproc

00:22:26.350 --> 00:22:28.274
is the ability to
bootstrap a cluster.

00:22:28.274 --> 00:22:30.190
Because we know you're
not just probably going

00:22:30.190 --> 00:22:33.214
to run MapReduce or
Spark in a vanilla form.

00:22:33.214 --> 00:22:35.380
You're probably going to
want to tweak those things.

00:22:35.380 --> 00:22:37.330
So not only in tuning
cluster parameters,

00:22:37.330 --> 00:22:41.260
but also the ability to install
things like Kafka, or Jupiter,

00:22:41.260 --> 00:22:44.170
or Zookeeper, or any other
kind of open source packages.

00:22:44.170 --> 00:22:46.720
We have a bunch of preexisting
bootstrap templates

00:22:46.720 --> 00:22:47.830
to do all that stuff.

00:22:47.830 --> 00:22:49.462
And then we give you
easy instructions

00:22:49.462 --> 00:22:51.670
on how to bootstrap it with
your own custom software.

00:22:51.670 --> 00:22:52.870
So if you've got
custom JAR files

00:22:52.870 --> 00:22:55.330
you want to spread across the
cluster, or custom applications

00:22:55.330 --> 00:22:56.890
that you've built,
you can push those out

00:22:56.890 --> 00:22:57.806
pretty easily as well.

00:23:00.472 --> 00:23:02.680
The one thing to take away,
that I would urge you all

00:23:02.680 --> 00:23:05.840
to take away from this
idea around Cloud Dataproc,

00:23:05.840 --> 00:23:09.740
is this transition from being
a cluster centric approach,

00:23:09.740 --> 00:23:11.690
to a very job centric approach.

00:23:11.690 --> 00:23:14.334
Instead of saying, listen, I
have a 100 node Hadoop cluster

00:23:14.334 --> 00:23:17.000
that everybody shares, and we're
all going to try to fit into it

00:23:17.000 --> 00:23:19.580
and make sure we can get all our
work done in a reasonable time,

00:23:19.580 --> 00:23:20.913
instead you shift your thinking.

00:23:20.913 --> 00:23:22.880
You turn it on its
head and you say,

00:23:22.880 --> 00:23:25.640
what if every job could
just have its own cluster?

00:23:25.640 --> 00:23:28.000
What if every job could run
as fast as they wanted to

00:23:28.000 --> 00:23:29.609
and get done super quickly?

00:23:29.609 --> 00:23:31.400
What could-- what kind
of impact could that

00:23:31.400 --> 00:23:34.665
have on your ability to use
your data in a meaningful way?

00:23:34.665 --> 00:23:36.290
So that's the idea
with Cloud Dataproc.

00:23:36.290 --> 00:23:38.450
We want people to move
away from this notion

00:23:38.450 --> 00:23:42.680
of having to run a single large
stateful cluster all the time,

00:23:42.680 --> 00:23:45.890
and instead make them move to
a very job centric approach.

00:23:45.890 --> 00:23:48.230
Because now you're letting
everybody scale themselves

00:23:48.230 --> 00:23:49.010
individually.

00:23:49.010 --> 00:23:51.093
And again, if you start
taking advantage of things

00:23:51.093 --> 00:23:53.840
like preemptable VMs,
custom VM sizing,

00:23:53.840 --> 00:23:56.756
you're getting the most
optimal deployment,

00:23:56.756 --> 00:23:58.130
the most optimal
compute cluster,

00:23:58.130 --> 00:23:59.604
for the needs of that job.

00:23:59.604 --> 00:24:00.770
And that's really important.

00:24:00.770 --> 00:24:02.270
And that has a lot
of impact, again,

00:24:02.270 --> 00:24:05.000
on what you could have
done in the past on-prem,

00:24:05.000 --> 00:24:06.710
with a limited
amount of resources,

00:24:06.710 --> 00:24:09.980
versus incredibly
elastic infrastructure

00:24:09.980 --> 00:24:12.847
inside of a cloud.

00:24:12.847 --> 00:24:14.430
All right, so that
was the second one.

00:24:14.430 --> 00:24:18.920
The third one is this idea
of exposing on-prem data

00:24:18.920 --> 00:24:20.635
to cloud applications.

00:24:20.635 --> 00:24:22.010
You want the cloud
applications--

00:24:22.010 --> 00:24:24.644
you want the part that
responds to customer requests,

00:24:24.644 --> 00:24:26.810
you want the parts that
interact with mobile devices

00:24:26.810 --> 00:24:29.810
or back of those services-- you
want those to be as responsive

00:24:29.810 --> 00:24:31.280
and scalable as possible.

00:24:31.280 --> 00:24:32.946
So very often we see
this where people

00:24:32.946 --> 00:24:34.820
want to deploy applications,
their developers

00:24:34.820 --> 00:24:36.860
want to be able to work
with new infrastructure,

00:24:36.860 --> 00:24:39.470
so they'll use Cloud to
deploy applications into.

00:24:39.470 --> 00:24:42.380
But they still need access
to data that's on-prem.

00:24:42.380 --> 00:24:45.160
So how do we facilitate that?

00:24:45.160 --> 00:24:46.840
So here's the
diagram, and I'll go

00:24:46.840 --> 00:24:49.440
through this in-depth
a little bit.

00:24:49.440 --> 00:24:51.930
So the first one is, you've
got to get the network

00:24:51.930 --> 00:24:53.790
infrastructure spun up.

00:24:53.790 --> 00:24:56.336
It's not just about having
good connectivity with all

00:24:56.336 --> 00:24:58.460
your applications talking
to each other in a cloud,

00:24:58.460 --> 00:25:01.230
or all your stuff that's sitting
on-prem talking to each other.

00:25:01.230 --> 00:25:02.940
You've got to facilitate
the connection between them.

00:25:02.940 --> 00:25:04.680
That's the first
most important step.

00:25:04.680 --> 00:25:06.940
And we do that with two
critical components.

00:25:06.940 --> 00:25:08.310
The first is Interconnect.

00:25:08.310 --> 00:25:10.830
Cloud Interconnect
let's basically, us,

00:25:10.830 --> 00:25:13.950
Google and y'all, find a
common third party partner,

00:25:13.950 --> 00:25:17.610
like an Equinix or an L3,
and facilitate low latency IP

00:25:17.610 --> 00:25:18.240
connectivity--

00:25:18.240 --> 00:25:19.597
IPv4 connectivity.

00:25:19.597 --> 00:25:21.180
That's the first,
most important step,

00:25:21.180 --> 00:25:23.130
because we want to maximize
the bandwidth between us.

00:25:23.130 --> 00:25:25.290
We don't want the network to
be the bottleneck if we're

00:25:25.290 --> 00:25:27.040
trying to front on
premise infrastructure,

00:25:27.040 --> 00:25:28.680
or on premise data.

00:25:28.680 --> 00:25:30.510
So we can get up to
like 10 gig links.

00:25:30.510 --> 00:25:32.280
Great, so we've solved
the latency problem

00:25:32.280 --> 00:25:33.540
once we do all that.

00:25:33.540 --> 00:25:35.220
What's the next important part?

00:25:35.220 --> 00:25:39.152
Securing that line, because that
is a public IPv4 connectivity.

00:25:39.152 --> 00:25:40.860
We can't just leave
that out in the open,

00:25:40.860 --> 00:25:43.443
because then we've got-- anybody
can look at that data passing

00:25:43.443 --> 00:25:44.092
through there.

00:25:44.092 --> 00:25:46.050
And we want to secure
that as best as possible,

00:25:46.050 --> 00:25:48.600
and make sure the only traffic
that we want to go through

00:25:48.600 --> 00:25:50.340
that pipe is ours on--

00:25:50.340 --> 00:25:51.840
yours and ours alone.

00:25:51.840 --> 00:25:53.240
So we do that with VPN.

00:25:53.240 --> 00:25:56.370
We have Cloud VPN on our side,
and we have a VPN gateway

00:25:56.370 --> 00:25:56.979
on your side.

00:25:56.979 --> 00:25:58.770
And we get those two
talking to each other,

00:25:58.770 --> 00:26:00.020
and it's a pretty simple step.

00:26:00.020 --> 00:26:03.660
We do this via the API, via
the gcloud tool, Deployment

00:26:03.660 --> 00:26:05.245
Manager, whatever
the case may be.

00:26:05.245 --> 00:26:06.870
It's a very simple
step to just spin up

00:26:06.870 --> 00:26:10.020
the VPN gateway on our side
and have it talk to yours.

00:26:10.020 --> 00:26:12.720
Once you've done that, we've
got a secure, low latency

00:26:12.720 --> 00:26:14.010
link between us.

00:26:14.010 --> 00:26:16.260
If you skip this step,
everything else kind of

00:26:16.260 --> 00:26:16.900
falls apart.

00:26:16.900 --> 00:26:18.526
So this is critical
and it's important.

00:26:18.526 --> 00:26:21.024
And it's kind of useful in a
lot of other scenarios as well.

00:26:21.024 --> 00:26:22.500
So it's important
to explore this

00:26:22.500 --> 00:26:26.160
as you're trying to make this
move into GCP in general.

00:26:26.160 --> 00:26:28.350
But after networking, the
next thing we want to do

00:26:28.350 --> 00:26:30.210
is front on premise data.

00:26:30.210 --> 00:26:32.910
I like using Kubernetes because
it lets me write applications

00:26:32.910 --> 00:26:34.493
in one place and
deploy them somewhere

00:26:34.493 --> 00:26:36.100
else on any Kubernetes cluster.

00:26:36.100 --> 00:26:37.770
So in this example,
I use Kubernetes.

00:26:37.770 --> 00:26:39.070
It could really be anything.

00:26:39.070 --> 00:26:42.570
But the idea is that
we've secured the link,

00:26:42.570 --> 00:26:44.847
we want to give new
applications access to the data.

00:26:44.847 --> 00:26:46.430
But we really don't
want to give them,

00:26:46.430 --> 00:26:48.650
like, direct, direct
access to the data.

00:26:48.650 --> 00:26:50.270
Let them go through a CRED API.

00:26:50.270 --> 00:26:52.017
Let those connections
be audited.

00:26:52.017 --> 00:26:53.600
Let those queries
and jobs be audited.

00:26:53.600 --> 00:26:55.340
Let them get logged very well.

00:26:55.340 --> 00:26:57.680
Basically, give
them a modern API

00:26:57.680 --> 00:27:00.374
on top of on-prem data
infrastructure that

00:27:00.374 --> 00:27:02.540
might be older or might be
newer, it doesn't matter.

00:27:02.540 --> 00:27:05.225
But give them a consistent
API to work off of.

00:27:05.225 --> 00:27:07.132
Let that front
your data sources.

00:27:07.132 --> 00:27:08.840
And it's important to
know to think about

00:27:08.840 --> 00:27:11.690
that, because one, certainly
follows a modern development

00:27:11.690 --> 00:27:15.260
path if you take this REST
API approach to the world.

00:27:15.260 --> 00:27:17.930
But also, as I said earlier,
it gives you an opportunity

00:27:17.930 --> 00:27:21.140
to monitor, very closely,
how that data is being used

00:27:21.140 --> 00:27:22.880
and where you can
make optimizations.

00:27:22.880 --> 00:27:25.130
If you give people direct
access to the database,

00:27:25.130 --> 00:27:27.507
you might not have as
much granular control

00:27:27.507 --> 00:27:28.340
as you want to have.

00:27:28.340 --> 00:27:30.740
Or you might be punting
the access control problem

00:27:30.740 --> 00:27:33.300
back to database, maybe to a
system that can't support it.

00:27:33.300 --> 00:27:35.120
So it's kind of
cleaner separation

00:27:35.120 --> 00:27:37.220
to have this little API,
this thin layer that

00:27:37.220 --> 00:27:40.250
sits-- a little shim that
sits between the database

00:27:40.250 --> 00:27:42.656
and new applications.

00:27:42.656 --> 00:27:44.530
And then the second
thing, or the next thing,

00:27:44.530 --> 00:27:46.697
is spinning up the cloud
application infrastructure.

00:27:46.697 --> 00:27:49.154
I picked Container Engineer,
but it can really be anything.

00:27:49.154 --> 00:27:50.770
It could be App
Engine, it could be

00:27:50.770 --> 00:27:53.300
applications running on
Compute Engine, doesn't matter.

00:27:53.300 --> 00:27:55.450
But the idea is
that, let us handle

00:27:55.450 --> 00:27:57.160
the scale part of the problem.

00:27:57.160 --> 00:28:01.300
Use our L7 load balancer
to get one global IP that

00:28:01.300 --> 00:28:02.830
works everywhere.

00:28:02.830 --> 00:28:05.380
Let that be the thing you give
out to DNS infrastructure.

00:28:05.380 --> 00:28:07.830
Send all your traffic
to those applications,

00:28:07.830 --> 00:28:10.144
and let us manage the
scale out of aspect.

00:28:10.144 --> 00:28:11.560
And then send
traffic back when we

00:28:11.560 --> 00:28:13.720
need to get access to that
on-prem infrastructure,

00:28:13.720 --> 00:28:16.442
that on-prem data.

00:28:16.442 --> 00:28:18.650
The last one-- and this is
sort of the optional step,

00:28:18.650 --> 00:28:20.002
it just depends.

00:28:20.002 --> 00:28:21.710
If you're inside of
Kubernetes and you're

00:28:21.710 --> 00:28:22.760
running Kubernetes
in both places,

00:28:22.760 --> 00:28:25.100
you might not really need
the service discovery piece

00:28:25.100 --> 00:28:26.810
with something like Console.

00:28:26.810 --> 00:28:30.630
Very often, the diagram
is not this simplistic.

00:28:30.630 --> 00:28:32.480
It's usually a litany
of applications

00:28:32.480 --> 00:28:35.030
on both sides of the
equation that may come and go

00:28:35.030 --> 00:28:37.430
and we may shift to the
approach that we're taking.

00:28:37.430 --> 00:28:40.940
So it is nice to have a good
cross cluster service discovery

00:28:40.940 --> 00:28:41.970
mechanism.

00:28:41.970 --> 00:28:43.220
And it's hard to beat Console.

00:28:43.220 --> 00:28:44.330
It works pretty well.

00:28:44.330 --> 00:28:46.229
It's got a pretty
solid following.

00:28:46.229 --> 00:28:47.020
So I'd like to do--

00:28:47.020 --> 00:28:50.270
I like to go on this
road pretty often.

00:28:50.270 --> 00:28:53.292
All right, so that was
the fronting on-prem data.

00:28:53.292 --> 00:28:54.750
The last one I want
to come through

00:28:54.750 --> 00:28:57.360
is scaling to meet
IoT workloads.

00:28:57.360 --> 00:29:01.212
This goes back to our
connected light bulb scenario.

00:29:01.212 --> 00:29:02.670
You've got all
these little devices

00:29:02.670 --> 00:29:04.500
that are generating a ton
of data, that are probably

00:29:04.500 --> 00:29:05.790
going to a local gateway.

00:29:05.790 --> 00:29:08.621
Or if you're familiar with
that Philips Hue system,

00:29:08.621 --> 00:29:10.620
they have light bulbs,
and they have the bridge,

00:29:10.620 --> 00:29:13.410
and the bridge is what
communicates out from the home.

00:29:13.410 --> 00:29:15.000
So in that sort of
a scenario, this

00:29:15.000 --> 00:29:16.125
is what it might look like.

00:29:16.125 --> 00:29:18.630
You might have the gateway
communicating with a REST API

00:29:18.630 --> 00:29:20.199
that's running on App Engine.

00:29:20.199 --> 00:29:21.990
Maybe you've got Cloud
Endpoints, maybe you

00:29:21.990 --> 00:29:23.070
don't, in front of it.

00:29:23.070 --> 00:29:25.907
But you're going to stream
data out from all those bulbs

00:29:25.907 --> 00:29:27.490
and you want to
capture all that data.

00:29:27.490 --> 00:29:29.100
But you want to
make sure, like we

00:29:29.100 --> 00:29:31.740
called at the bottom, that
on premise infrastructure

00:29:31.740 --> 00:29:33.070
still has access to that data.

00:29:33.070 --> 00:29:34.560
You don't want to lock
it out, because you're

00:29:34.560 --> 00:29:36.060
locking out your
ability for everyone

00:29:36.060 --> 00:29:37.060
to take advantage of it.

00:29:39.570 --> 00:29:41.669
So what does that really--
how do we do that?

00:29:41.669 --> 00:29:43.710
Part of the reason you
want to go down this road,

00:29:43.710 --> 00:29:46.126
again, it kind of goes back
to the whole on-prem challenge

00:29:46.126 --> 00:29:48.249
piece, which is, you've
maxed out resources,

00:29:48.249 --> 00:29:49.790
you might not have
any more headroom,

00:29:49.790 --> 00:29:51.170
and it takes too
long, even if you

00:29:51.170 --> 00:29:52.940
were able to funnel
all that data through,

00:29:52.940 --> 00:29:55.580
because of just generic
compute limitations.

00:29:55.580 --> 00:29:58.340
So you want to try to find
the best approach here

00:29:58.340 --> 00:30:01.220
that again, lets you take
advantage of all the elasticity

00:30:01.220 --> 00:30:03.080
and the scalability
on the cloud side,

00:30:03.080 --> 00:30:05.630
but doesn't cut off people
from learning from that data

00:30:05.630 --> 00:30:07.550
as quickly as possible.

00:30:07.550 --> 00:30:11.080
So the first, most critical
component here is Pub/Sub.

00:30:11.080 --> 00:30:13.757
How many of you guys have
heard of PubSub already today?

00:30:13.757 --> 00:30:14.340
Oh, everybody.

00:30:14.340 --> 00:30:16.007
So I could just skip
the slide, right?

00:30:16.007 --> 00:30:17.840
I'm going to talk about
this really quickly.

00:30:17.840 --> 00:30:20.660
Think of Pub/Sub as a
completely managed--

00:30:20.660 --> 00:30:21.740
forget the diagram.

00:30:21.740 --> 00:30:25.430
It is a completely managed
message delivery mechanism.

00:30:25.430 --> 00:30:27.720
We can create things like
topics and subscriptions.

00:30:27.720 --> 00:30:30.470
Subscriptions can be pull or
push based, depending on what

00:30:30.470 --> 00:30:32.630
the downstream endpoint is.

00:30:32.630 --> 00:30:35.840
And if you think about messages
coming in to infrastructure,

00:30:35.840 --> 00:30:37.340
we can actually
split those messages

00:30:37.340 --> 00:30:39.800
and have multiple different
mechanisms handle them.

00:30:39.800 --> 00:30:42.300
So you might have one mechanism
that's sitting there saying,

00:30:42.300 --> 00:30:44.030
I'm going to aggregate
all this data in flight

00:30:44.030 --> 00:30:45.380
and I'll write it to a database.

00:30:45.380 --> 00:30:47.421
Another one might say,
I'm going to ETL this data

00:30:47.421 --> 00:30:48.820
and write it to
a data warehouse.

00:30:48.820 --> 00:30:51.564
A third one might say, I'm just
going to pipe these things back

00:30:51.564 --> 00:30:53.480
out to another Pub/Sub
topic and send messages

00:30:53.480 --> 00:30:54.552
to another application.

00:30:54.552 --> 00:30:56.510
You can do all that with
Pub/Sub with, I think,

00:30:56.510 --> 00:30:58.460
about three or four
gcloud commands.

00:30:58.460 --> 00:30:59.730
Create the topic.

00:30:59.730 --> 00:31:01.130
Create a series
of subscriptions.

00:31:01.130 --> 00:31:03.650
Each one of them gets their
own copy of the message.

00:31:03.650 --> 00:31:05.007
So it's a pretty powerful piece.

00:31:05.007 --> 00:31:07.340
And then on top of that, we
have other cool, interesting

00:31:07.340 --> 00:31:09.230
features, like labeling.

00:31:09.230 --> 00:31:11.157
You can auto expire
certain things by default.

00:31:11.157 --> 00:31:12.740
I think by default,
in a subscription,

00:31:12.740 --> 00:31:14.464
messages expire in
about seven days.

00:31:14.464 --> 00:31:16.880
If you want to tune that, you
can have the ability to tune

00:31:16.880 --> 00:31:18.060
that as well.

00:31:18.060 --> 00:31:20.700
And in fact, as of I
think a few months ago,

00:31:20.700 --> 00:31:23.120
Pub/Sub actually
connects up to Kafka.

00:31:23.120 --> 00:31:25.550
So if you've got a Kafka
cluster running on premise,

00:31:25.550 --> 00:31:27.710
you can actually hook
up to Kafka ingest,

00:31:27.710 --> 00:31:29.319
and as an output source as well.

00:31:29.319 --> 00:31:31.610
So you can actually use that
as a mechanism to get data

00:31:31.610 --> 00:31:32.270
in and out.

00:31:32.270 --> 00:31:35.030
And we do this very
often with customers.

00:31:35.030 --> 00:31:37.610
Pub/Sub has a really
easy to use REST API.

00:31:37.610 --> 00:31:39.440
That means there
are a ton of tools,

00:31:39.440 --> 00:31:41.840
like the Enterprise
Service bus tools

00:31:41.840 --> 00:31:43.460
of the world, that
are really critical

00:31:43.460 --> 00:31:44.810
to your infrastructure.

00:31:44.810 --> 00:31:47.060
Those can actually just
access a REST endpoint as long

00:31:47.060 --> 00:31:48.290
as you set them up correctly.

00:31:48.290 --> 00:31:49.831
So they're actually
going and hitting

00:31:49.831 --> 00:31:52.310
Pub/Sub to get information
back out in a way that's good

00:31:52.310 --> 00:31:56.380
and sort of scalable
for them as well.

00:31:56.380 --> 00:31:59.340
So the idea here is that we
want to reduce time to insight.

00:31:59.340 --> 00:32:01.264
So first, we're going
to have events coming in

00:32:01.264 --> 00:32:02.180
off those light bulbs.

00:32:02.180 --> 00:32:03.920
They're going to
hit the IoT gateway.

00:32:03.920 --> 00:32:05.670
The IoT gateway will
send them to the API.

00:32:05.670 --> 00:32:08.080
And the API will start
this chain of events.

00:32:08.080 --> 00:32:09.930
So what will first
happen is, those events

00:32:09.930 --> 00:32:13.680
get pushed individually to
Pub/Sub as individual messages.

00:32:13.680 --> 00:32:16.500
Then we'll have an Apache Beam
pipeline that's running inside

00:32:16.500 --> 00:32:20.070
of Dataflow, take in every bit
of those streaming messages.

00:32:20.070 --> 00:32:22.410
And because it's in
Dataflow, that Beam pipeline

00:32:22.410 --> 00:32:25.260
can grow or scale as
much as it needs to,

00:32:25.260 --> 00:32:28.530
and we can shard, reshard, and
reshard that data in flight,

00:32:28.530 --> 00:32:31.250
so that we're not dropping
anything on the floor.

00:32:31.250 --> 00:32:33.690
And that Dataflow job is
actually going to do a split.

00:32:33.690 --> 00:32:35.874
It's going to write things
out into two places.

00:32:35.874 --> 00:32:37.290
The first thing
it'll do is, it'll

00:32:37.290 --> 00:32:39.780
do maybe a little bit of
light ETL transformation

00:32:39.780 --> 00:32:41.340
and kick that stuff
over to BigQuery,

00:32:41.340 --> 00:32:44.730
if you want to do kind of SQL on
an ad hoc or exploratory basis

00:32:44.730 --> 00:32:46.200
later on, or if
you want to build

00:32:46.200 --> 00:32:49.476
a compelling visualization
or a report of that data.

00:32:49.476 --> 00:32:50.850
And then what we
might also do is

00:32:50.850 --> 00:32:54.607
write the very raw time series
and use Bigtable for that.

00:32:54.607 --> 00:32:56.190
The idea of splitting
these things out

00:32:56.190 --> 00:32:57.780
is really just by use case.

00:32:57.780 --> 00:33:01.620
We might want to do business
analysis in BigQuery.

00:33:01.620 --> 00:33:04.320
We might want to use the
Bigtable storage view, the time

00:33:04.320 --> 00:33:08.040
series view, as a source for
longer doing machine learning

00:33:08.040 --> 00:33:09.700
or experimental use cases.

00:33:09.700 --> 00:33:12.570
So it's nice to have the option
of not throwing anything away,

00:33:12.570 --> 00:33:15.360
not over normalizing the data.

00:33:15.360 --> 00:33:16.830
We have got a lot of options.

00:33:16.830 --> 00:33:18.655
Storage is relatively
cheap these days.

00:33:18.655 --> 00:33:20.280
Let's write it out
in two places and we

00:33:20.280 --> 00:33:21.835
can take advantage of that.

00:33:21.835 --> 00:33:24.210
And then the last step is,
the Dataflow job will actually

00:33:24.210 --> 00:33:28.230
do a third output and push
things like aggregates.

00:33:28.230 --> 00:33:31.170
You might not need to
know every single event

00:33:31.170 --> 00:33:33.150
that every single
light generated

00:33:33.150 --> 00:33:35.400
from every single home
that they're deployed in.

00:33:35.400 --> 00:33:37.980
What you might want to do
is roll up maybe a home,

00:33:37.980 --> 00:33:41.850
and say, hey, for this house,
we saw the bulbs change

00:33:41.850 --> 00:33:45.240
transition-- or transition from
state to state, maybe n times,

00:33:45.240 --> 00:33:47.760
And wrap that up as a single
message, one single piece

00:33:47.760 --> 00:33:49.710
of data, like a
single piece of JSON,

00:33:49.710 --> 00:33:52.300
and then publish
that to Pub/Sub.

00:33:52.300 --> 00:33:54.210
And then you can
have other tools

00:33:54.210 --> 00:33:56.130
like the on premise
infrastructure read

00:33:56.130 --> 00:33:58.747
from that Pub/Sub topic and
just get the aggregates out.

00:33:58.747 --> 00:34:01.080
So now you can start to take
advantage of the idea that,

00:34:01.080 --> 00:34:03.090
listen we're going to
offload all the scale

00:34:03.090 --> 00:34:05.280
and ingest part of
this problem to Cloud,

00:34:05.280 --> 00:34:07.500
but still have access to
the data, pull it back out,

00:34:07.500 --> 00:34:09.070
and learn something from it.

00:34:09.070 --> 00:34:12.020
And it may go back into other
on premise applications or tools

00:34:12.020 --> 00:34:14.699
that are maybe internal facing
or even external facing.

00:34:14.699 --> 00:34:16.620
But it gives you the
ability to kind of assess

00:34:16.620 --> 00:34:19.826
what's happening in these
houses on a regular basis

00:34:19.826 --> 00:34:20.909
without losing any access.

00:34:23.500 --> 00:34:25.755
So, we kind of went
through four scenarios.

00:34:25.755 --> 00:34:27.130
We burned through
it a little bit

00:34:27.130 --> 00:34:29.406
quickly because I do
talk fast, I apologize.

00:34:29.406 --> 00:34:30.739
But I'm happy to take questions.

00:34:30.739 --> 00:34:32.949
I do want to come to that.

00:34:32.949 --> 00:34:35.230
As you think about, how
do we get started here?

00:34:35.230 --> 00:34:36.610
There's a lot of detail.

00:34:36.610 --> 00:34:38.110
There's a lot of little things.

00:34:38.110 --> 00:34:40.810
If you take nothing
else away from today,

00:34:40.810 --> 00:34:44.770
don't hesitate to really go
down the path of understanding

00:34:44.770 --> 00:34:46.210
network connectivity.

00:34:46.210 --> 00:34:48.190
If you don't get that
network part of it right,

00:34:48.190 --> 00:34:50.039
everything goes real slow.

00:34:50.039 --> 00:34:52.330
And it's really hard to take
advantage of all the scale

00:34:52.330 --> 00:34:54.797
when something over here
can run really, really fast,

00:34:54.797 --> 00:34:57.130
and you might have enough
infrastructure that runs fast,

00:34:57.130 --> 00:34:59.920
but the pipe between
the two is slow,

00:34:59.920 --> 00:35:02.980
or you're dropping packets,
or it's not secure.

00:35:02.980 --> 00:35:04.390
That's a critical part.

00:35:04.390 --> 00:35:06.400
The network is very,
very important,

00:35:06.400 --> 00:35:08.240
as you think about it.

00:35:08.240 --> 00:35:10.390
And then find a workload
that needs scale.

00:35:10.390 --> 00:35:12.460
I'm pretty positive that
every single one of you

00:35:12.460 --> 00:35:15.144
could come, stand up
and tell me at least one

00:35:15.144 --> 00:35:16.560
workload that
you've run into that

00:35:16.560 --> 00:35:18.910
is running out of scale
in the current approach.

00:35:18.910 --> 00:35:21.820
You need more
headroom to go faster.

00:35:21.820 --> 00:35:23.254
So then you've got
to think about,

00:35:23.254 --> 00:35:24.670
how do we actually
make that move?

00:35:24.670 --> 00:35:25.794
Do we have to migrate data?

00:35:25.794 --> 00:35:27.430
Do we have to
migrate applications?

00:35:27.430 --> 00:35:30.400
Do we have to point data
that's coming in to Cloud

00:35:30.400 --> 00:35:32.350
and have it go
through that approach?

00:35:32.350 --> 00:35:33.310
But you've got to
find something that

00:35:33.310 --> 00:35:35.590
makes sense and figure out
what your migration strategy is

00:35:35.590 --> 00:35:36.490
going to look like.

00:35:36.490 --> 00:35:38.830
And then you want to test
and scale it out, and then

00:35:38.830 --> 00:35:41.830
figure out which metrics
or which key PIs are really

00:35:41.830 --> 00:35:43.990
important to understand
and say, this

00:35:43.990 --> 00:35:47.020
is successful or not successful,
or we have to iterate.

00:35:47.020 --> 00:35:49.390
And then the last thing is
really, frankly, be agile.

00:35:49.390 --> 00:35:53.320
Be open to shifting the approach
and finding new ways to do it.

00:35:53.320 --> 00:35:56.020
But use mechanisms that are
really easy to iterate around.

00:35:56.020 --> 00:35:58.140
Things like Pub/Sub
and Cloud storage

00:35:58.140 --> 00:36:00.340
are super easy to
work with because you

00:36:00.340 --> 00:36:02.285
can put data in a
pretty generic way,

00:36:02.285 --> 00:36:03.910
and then you have a
lot of capabilities

00:36:03.910 --> 00:36:06.580
to move things around and
reformat what those look like.

00:36:09.194 --> 00:36:10.610
A couple of other
sessions I would

00:36:10.610 --> 00:36:13.790
suggest that you guys consider
looking into as you're here

00:36:13.790 --> 00:36:15.050
today and tomorrow.

00:36:15.050 --> 00:36:17.570
So one is open source
data processing on GCP.

00:36:17.570 --> 00:36:20.210
That's going to go into
depth on Beam and Hadoop

00:36:20.210 --> 00:36:23.300
and Spark running
on Google Cloud.

00:36:23.300 --> 00:36:25.801
The next one is migrating data
warehouse to Google BigQuery.

00:36:25.801 --> 00:36:27.050
That's a really important one.

00:36:27.050 --> 00:36:29.120
That kind of dovetails
right on the first example

00:36:29.120 --> 00:36:31.730
we talked about, migrating
on-prem relational

00:36:31.730 --> 00:36:34.190
to Cloud Data Warehouse.

00:36:34.190 --> 00:36:36.380
And then the last one is
large scale data ingest.

00:36:36.380 --> 00:36:40.080
This is true for applications,
mobile devices, IoT,

00:36:40.080 --> 00:36:41.000
what have you.

00:36:41.000 --> 00:36:42.660
If you have a lot
of data coming in,

00:36:42.660 --> 00:36:45.970
how do you do it,
again, in-depth on GCP?

